<h2>A scheme, a draw, and a clerical slip</h2>
<p>The petitioner put her money into DDA&rsquo;s New Pattern Registration Scheme in 1979 and waited twenty-three years for the draw that finally selected her on 31 May 2002 for an MIG flat in Dwarka. Then the machinery failed at its simplest step: the demand-cum-allotment letter was addressed to &ldquo;Khanpur&rdquo;, a different locality with a different PIN, instead of her recorded &ldquo;Kham Pur&rdquo; address. It returned undelivered. So did the cancellation letter of 10 February 2004. For six more years she knew nothing, until a DDA public hearing in May 2010 revealed both the allotment and its cancellation. A representation followed within a week, and the writ within the year.</p>

<h2>The three defences, and why each failed</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <strong>DDA&rsquo;s objections</strong>
    <p>The dispute is contractual, so a writ does not lie; the petition is barred by delay and laches; the demand was dispatched and publicly notified, so non-payment was default.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <strong>The court&rsquo;s answers</strong>
    <p>State action in allotment must be fair, and its fairness is examinable under Article 226 following <em>ABL International</em>; delay runs from knowledge in 2010, not dispatch in 2003-04; and a letter to the wrong address earns no presumption of service under Section 27 of the General Clauses Act, while newspaper notices cannot replace individual notice.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>The remedy&rsquo;s careful balance</h2>
<p>Restitution after two decades cannot simply hand over a 2002 flat at 2002 prices. The court&rsquo;s formula: a mini draw for a similar MIG Dwarka flat, with a fresh demand-cum-allotment letter at the rate prevailing on 8 March 2011 &mdash; the date the petitioner invoked the court&rsquo;s jurisdiction &mdash; payable within four weeks. The allottee bears the price as it stood when she acted diligently; she is not punished for the years the error lay undiscovered, nor rewarded with vintage pricing for the litigation years.</p>

<h2>Why the case matters beyond DDA</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Address accuracy is a legal duty.</strong> Housing authorities, societies and lenders who serve demands and cancellations must transcribe recorded addresses exactly; the presumption of service collapses with the first wrong word.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Knowledge starts the clock.</strong> For writ limitation and laches, courts look at when the citizen learnt of the adverse action, especially where the authority&rsquo;s own communication failure caused the ignorance.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Public notice is a supplement, not a substitute.</strong> Gazette and newspaper publication protects process in mass schemes, but individual rights are extinguished only by individual notice.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This report summarises a judgment of the Delhi High Court dated 25 November 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice; outcomes in allotment-cancellation disputes depend on each scheme&rsquo;s terms and the service record.</div>
